<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jacques Necker</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1732">1732</a>–<a href="/years/1804">1804</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jacques Necker was a Genevan banker and statesman who served as finance minister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xvi">Louis XVI</a>. Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a> in 1732, he grew rich as a banker in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and in 1777 was made director-general of the royal treasury, as his Protestant faith barred him from the post of Controller-General. In 1781 he published the Compte rendu, making the royal budget public, and was dismissed within months. Recalled in 1788 amid fiscal crisis, he doubled the representation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-estate">Third Estate</a> at the Estates-General; his dismissal on 11 July 1789 helped bring about the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/storming-of-the-bastille">Storming of the Bastille</a>. Recalled within two days, he resigned in September 1790 and died in 1804.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jacques-necker.jpg" alt="Jacques Necker" width="330" height="381" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: After Joseph-Siffred Duplessis · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Necker,_Jacques_-_Duplessis.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Jacques Necker was born on 30 September 1732 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a>, the son of Karl Friedrich Necker, a lawyer from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kostrzyn-nad-odra">Küstrin</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> who became professor of public law at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/academy-of-geneva">Academy of Geneva</a>. Necker moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1748 and became a clerk in the bank of Isaac Vernet and Peter Thellusson. A series of trades on the stock exchange brought him a quick profit of half a million livres, and in 1762 he became a partner in the bank. In 1764 he married Suzanne Curchod, a Swiss governess once engaged to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-gibbon">Edward Gibbon</a>; their daughter became the author Madame de Staël.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In his first term Necker refused a salary. He tried to divide the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taille">taille</a> and the capitation more equally, abolished more than five hundred sinecures and financed the French debt by loans at high interest rather than by new taxes. In April 1778 he paid 2.4 million livres from his own fortune into the treasury. His provincial assemblies, with equal numbers from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-estate">Third Estate</a>, succeeded only in Berry and Haute-Guyenne.</p><p>The Compte rendu au roi of 1781 summarised income and expenditure and was the first record of royal finances made public; some 200,000 copies were sold. Critics accused him of brightening the picture by leaving out military outlays and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-debt">national debt</a>. When Necker demanded a seat on the royal council, Maurepas and Vergennes threatened to resign, and he was dismissed on 19 May 1781.</p><p>On 25 or 26 August 1788 he was called back to office, this time insisting on the title of Controller-General and a place on the royal council. In the winter of 1788–89 he arranged grain for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a> bank Hope &amp; Co., using the 2.4 million livres in the treasury as collateral.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>At the Estates-General on 5 May 1789 Necker spoke for three hours, though a secretary read most of the address; he urged moderation and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a> of the English type. By September 1789 the treasury was empty, and in December the Constituent Assembly voted the first issue of assignats secured on confiscated church lands. He resigned in September 1790 to general indifference and was allowed to leave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> on 11 September.</p><p>He spent his last years at Coppet Castle writing on political economy and law. His house and two million livres were confiscated after he was placed on the list of émigrés. He died on 9 April 1804. Necker Hospital for Children in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> is named after him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jacques Necker” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1779 the reforms of <b>Jacques Necker</b>, the director-general of finances, abolished serfdom on the crown’s own lands, but not on those of private lords or the church.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ancien-regime-france">Social Hierarchy of Ancien Régime France</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=46.2,6.15&amp;year=1778" aria-label="Geneva on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4695 538 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Jacques Necker"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5145 808h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5145 808h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=46.2,6.15&amp;year=1778">What was here in 1778? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
